Let G be an abelian Hausdorff topological group. The dual group G^ of G is the group of all continuous homomorphisms from G into the circle group T = R/Z, equipped with the compact-open topology. The second dual G^^ is defined analogously as the dual of G^. There is a canonical homomorphism α_G : G → G^^ given by α_G(x)(χ) = χ(x). The group G is called reflexive if α_G is a topological group isomorphism (i.e. an algebraic isomorphism that is also a homeomorphism). A topological group H is called separable if it has a countable dense subset, and metrizable if its topology is induced by a metric. A quotient group of G is the group G/N equipped with the quotient topology, where N is a closed normal subgroup of G.

Question. Does every infinite reflexive abelian topological group G admit a Hausdorff quotient group G/N that is:
(i) non-trivial and separable;
(ii) infinite and separable;
(iii) separable and metrizable;
(iv) infinite, separable, and metrizable?

